ine.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build dict of node by id.\n- Build parent->children map from nodes.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Set of required symbols.\n- Initially all node ids are retained.\n- Process nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node id string ascending.\n- For each node id:\n  - If node is not retained? skip (already removed)\n  - Check conditions: node['removable'] must be True, node['parent'] is not None (not root), node['symbol'] not in required_symbols.\n  - Check if node has any retained children: i.e., any child id in current retained set. If yes, skip.\n  - If all conditions satisfied, then tentatively remove: but also need to check that after removal, all required symbols still have at least one representative. Since we already ensure we only remove nodes with non-required symbols, that is automatically satisfied? But consider if a required symbol appears only in nodes that are not removable? It's fine. However, if a required symbol appears only in nodes that are removable? That can't happen because we never remove them. So it's safe. But to be rigorous, we could check that the set of symbols of retained nodes still contains all required symbols after removal. Given we never remove a node with required symbol, it's fine. But we'll include the check anyway.\n  - If all good, remove node: remove from retained set, record removal order.\n- After processing all, gather retained IDs, sort them (by id string? It says sorted retained IDs – presumably ascending string order or maybe by depth? The problem doesn't specify sort order, but likely alphabetical/numeric ID order). We'll sort by id string.\n- Return dictionary with \"retained_ids\" list and \"removal_order\" list.\n\nNote: The node IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Sorting ascending naturally works.\n\nWe must not mutate the case. So we work on a copy.\n\nImplementation: define `solve(case)` that takes the case dict (with payload containing nodes list and required_symbols). Extract payload. Build data structures. Then process.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object.\n\nWe need to output the Python source as a string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we write the Python code, then encode it as a JSON string (escaped). The entire response must be a single JSON object.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The nodes list may not be in order; we need to build parent-child mapping. Depth may be given but we can also compute from parent but it's given. Use depth for sorting.\n\nThe \"removable\" flag is boolean. Children: we consider only direct children.\n\nStable tie break: node ID as string.\n\nProcessing order: we should consider nodes in that order, but if a node is removed later, its parent's \"retained children\" condition changes. Since we process deep first, we remove leaf nodes; then when we later consider parent, it may no longer have retained children. But careful: if we remove a child, we update retained set. When we later consider the parent, we check its retained children at that moment. That's correct.\n\nEdge case: a node that is removable but has children that are also removable but not processed yet? Since we process deeper first, children are processed before parent. So if a child is removed, parent's retained children condition may change. That's fine.\n\nNow implement.\n\nReturn value: let's assume we need to return {\"retained_ids\": sorted list, \"removal_order\": list in order of removal}. Also include maybe \"task_id\"? No, just the result.\n\nWe'll produce Python code that defines solve(case). Use only standard library.\n\nLet's write."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1737,"completion_tokens":3775,"total_tokens":5512,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3349},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":73},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}